@nirname @subhash-halder From what I had to go through to keep backwards compatibility while fixing the bugs, I would argue against pushing text states without  demarcating them with something (similar to how string are demarcated with quotation marks). For now, what's in this PR works because the `title` token has the highest precedence. However, say that we wanted to add inline styling or anything after the title text, without giving it too much precedence it would be impossible. 

E.g., imagine we were going to implement inline styling in the future, similar to what is seen in classDiagram. It would be impossible to do something like  `title some text:::styleclass` without giving the `:::` token higher precedence, because the alphanum/text token can go on until it hits a newline. This would not be a problem if we only used strings or markdown strings for title text, because as soon as we read the end quotation mark, we can do anything afterwards

So, would giving a token like `:::` relatively high precedence? No one knows the answer to that because we can't predict the type of features that we'll want to add in the future. Overall, this is a call to the team members.

