@ibrahimWassouf good point. There was `data_inner`, but its action was replacing `data` with `data_inner` rather than pushing new state on top of it. It is my fault, my comments were not clear enough.

To clarify - we are scanning NUMBER_WITH_DECIMAL within 2 different states:
`axis_data` and `data_inner`.
We can leave only one regexp for numbers by assigning a new state:
```
<axis_data>"[" { this.pushState('inner_data'); return 'SQUARE_BRACES_START'; }
<data>"[" { this.pushState('inner_data'); return 'SQUARE_BRACES_START'; }
...
<inner_data>regexp for numbers { return 'NUMBER_WITH_DECIMAL'; }
```

Do we need to `popState` here? I'll try to explain. The majority of languages can be recursively structured. Thus it can be processed with a loop and stack. When we open a new structure we usually push a new state. When we are closing the structure we usually pop the state out of the stack.
```
"[" { this.pushState('some_data'); return 'SQUARE_BRACES_START' }
<some_data>number_regexp { return 'NUMBER' }
<some_data>"]" { this.popState('some_data'); return SQUARE_BRACES_END' }
```
That is simply an example of what the usual tree-like structure looks like

